1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,619
Two thousand years ago,

2
00:00:01,669 --> 00:00:04,268
someone built a computer
from bronze gears.

3
00:00:04,618 --> 00:00:06,237
No electricity.

4
00:00:06,337 --> 00:00:06,976
No screen.

5
00:00:07,126 --> 00:00:08,216
Just a hand-crank—

6
00:00:08,256 --> 00:00:11,099
and a machine designed
to model the sky.

7
00:00:11,449 --> 00:00:14,347
Recovered from a shipwreck
near the Greek island

8
00:00:14,387 --> 00:00:16,211
of Antikythera in 1901,

9
00:00:16,251 --> 00:00:18,905
its corroded fragments
concealed a device

10
00:00:18,945 --> 00:00:21,543
probably built between
150 and 100 BCE.

11
00:00:21,893 --> 00:00:22,708
Turn the crank,

12
00:00:22,748 --> 00:00:23,932
and its dials could track

13
00:00:23,972 --> 00:00:26,011
the cycles of the
Sun and Moon,

14
00:00:26,051 --> 00:00:27,845
follow complex calendars,

15
00:00:27,885 --> 00:00:29,504
and predict eclipses.

16
00:00:29,854 --> 00:00:33,187
It is known as the
Antikythera Mechanism—

17
00:00:33,237 --> 00:00:36,080
the oldest known
analogue computer.

18
00:00:36,430 --> 00:00:39,028
Its complete design
remains debated.

19
00:00:39,178 --> 00:00:42,566
But the surviving machine
shows how sophisticated

20
00:00:42,606 --> 00:00:45,000
ancient Greek engineering
could be.
